%K Nucleus Accumbens
%K Rats
%K Niacin
%K Nicotine
%X Prenatal stress (PS) can induce several long-lasting behavioral and molecular abnormalities in rats. It can also be considered as a risk factor for many psychiatric diseases like schizophrenia depression or PTSD and predispose to addiction. In this study we investigated the effect of prenatal stress on the reinforcing properties of nicotine in the CPP paradigm. Then we examined the mRNA expression of the D2 dopaminergic receptors using the quantitative real-time PCR technique in the nucleus accumbens (NAcc). We found that prenatally stressed rats exhibited a greater place preference for the nicotine-paired compartment than the control rats. Moreover we observed an overexpression of the DRD2 gene in adult offspring stressed in utero and a downregulation in the PS NIC group (PS rats treated with nicotine) compared with their control counterparts (C NIC). These data suggest that maternal stress can permanently alter the offspring's addictive behavior and D2 receptors' expression.
%R doi:10.1016/j.neuroscience.2015.07.029

