%K Infant, Newborn
%K Rats
%K Dopamine
%X Rat pups were individually isolated from the mother and nest for 1 h/day from postnatal days (PND) 2 to 9 and tested as juveniles (PND 26-30) compared to nonhandled (NH) controls. In response to 1 h of restraint stress NH rats increased locomotor activity and dopamine (DA) levels but neonatally isolated (ISO) rats did not. Both groups had increased plasma corticosterone levels in response to restraint but corticosterone levels were higher in ISO than in NH. Brain allopregnanolone (3alpha5alpha-THP) levels also increased in response to stress but NH and ISO did not differ. Sex of the rats was not a factor for any of the measures except plasma corticosterone levels where females had higher levels than males. These data indicate that the effects of neonatal isolation persist postweaning and that the effects are most evident in response to stress as opposed to under baseline conditions.
%R doi:10.1016/s0091-3057(02)00758-x

