%A Usui N., Ono Y., Aramaki R., Berto S., Konopka G., Matsuzaki H., Shimada S.
%K BZ-X700camerafluorescence microscopeinfrared sensorpolymerase chain reaction systemQuantStudio 7 FlexSCANET MV-40transcriptomeadolescentanimal experimentanimal modelanxietyarticlecontrolled studycytoarchitectureearly life stressfeces analysisfluorescence microscopygene expressionhistologyimmunohistochemistrylight-dark testmalemarble burying testmental stressmicrogliamousenerve cellnonhumanopen field testprefrontal cortexreal time polymerase chain reactionRNA sequencingsocial behaviorsocial interactionsocial interaction testsocial isolationsocial preference
%K Gene Expression
%K Anxiety
%X Early life stress (ELS) such as abuse neglect and maltreatment exhibits a strong impact on the brain and mental development of children. However it is not fully understood how ELS affects social behaviors and social-associated behaviors as well as developing prefrontal cortex (PFC). In this study we performed social isolation on weaned pre-adolescent mice until adolescence and investigated these behaviors and PFC characteristics in adolescent mice. We found the ELS induced social impairments in social novelty social interaction and social preference in adolescent mice. We also observed increases of anxiety-like behaviors in ELS mice. In histological analysis we found a reduced number of neurons and an increased number of microglia in the PFC of ELS mice. To identify the gene associated with behavioral and histological features we analyzed transcriptome in the PFC of ELS mice and identified 15 differentially expressed genes involved in transcriptional regulation stress and synaptic signaling. Our study demonstrates that ELS influences social behaviors anxiety-like behaviors through cytoarchitectural and transcriptomic alterations in the PFC of adolescent mice.
%R doi:10.3389/fgene.2021.754198

