%T Lycium barbarum polysaccharide attenuates emotional injury of offspring elicited by prenatal chronic stress in rats via regulation of gut microbiota
%D 2021
%8 2021
%J Biomedicine and Pharmacotherapy
%V 143
%A Zhao F., Guan S., Fu Y., Wang K., Liu Z., Ng T. B.
%K cortisol test kitDNA purification kitE.Z.N.A.electronic balanceELISA kitgenetic analyzerMiSeq PE300polymerase chain reaction systemspectrophotometerultraviolet spectrophotometercorticosteroneLycium barbarum extractpolysaccharideRNA 16Sadultanimal experimentanimal modelarticleBacteroidetesbody weightchemical compositionchronic unpredictable stresscontrolled studycorticosterone blood leveldepressionenzyme linked immunosorbent assayfeces analysisfemaleFirmicutesintestine floraLactobacillusmalemetabolitemicrobial diversitymothernewbornnonhumanopen field testpregnancyprenatal stressprogenyProteobacteriapsychotraumaratsucrose preference testtail suspension testVerrucomicrobiaGeneAmp 9700MiSeqNanoDrop 2000
%K Polysaccharides
%K Rats
%X Stress during pregnancy is not only detrimental to a woman's own physical and mental health but can also cause changes in the intrauterine environment and even have an impact on later growth and development this study was designed to understand the changes of gut microbiota in the maternal and offspring caused by prenatal chronic stress and to explore the regulatory effect of LBP on gut microbiota and then to improve the emotional damage caused by prenatal chronic stress in the offspring. A rat model of prenatal chronic stress was made and used LBP to intervene by gavage. Fresh feces of offspring were collected the concentration of microbial metabolites were tested by ELISA. Illumina MiSeqPE300 sequencing technology was used to determine the sequence of 16S rRNA V3-V4 of microorganisms. On the PND 42 the emotional function of offspring were tested by open-field test (OFT) sucrose preference test (SPT) and tail of suspend test (TST). Results indicated that stress factors increased the plasma corticosterone level of rats during pregnancy and they appeared depressive behaviors. The body weight of offspring during prenatal chronic stress was lower than the control group and the plasma corticosterone level was increased. Prenatal chronic stress had a significant impact on emotional performance of the offspring on OFT SPT and TST. Alpha diversity of gut microbiota and microbiota composition in offspring of prenatal chronic stress was attenuated and some relationships existed between these parameters. LBP treatment reduced offspring's plasma corticosterone level and improved their body weight changed the emotional function increased the diversity of gut microbiota. Collectively these findings disclose that prenatal chronic stress not only causes emotional injury on the offspring but also changes the gut microbiota of the mother and offspring; LBP may regulate the intestinal flora of the mother then reducing the influence of stress factors on the emotional injury of offspring.
%R doi:10.1016/j.biopha.2021.112087

