%T Maternal separation leads to regional hippocampal microglial activation and alters the behavior in the adolescence in a sex-specific manner
%D 2020
%8 2020
%J Brain Behavior and Immunity - Health
%V 9
%A Bachiller S., Paulus A., Vázquez-Reyes S., García-Domínguez I., Deierborg T.
%K RNA5ht6r geneadolescenceadolescentanimal experimentanimal modelanimal tissueanxietyarticlebehaviorbrain tissuecell activationcontrolled studyCox 2 genedentate gyrusdepressionelevated plus maze testfemalegenegene expressionhippocampal CA1 regionhippocampal CA3 regionimage analysisimmunofluorescencemalematernal deprivationmicrogliamousenerve cell plasticitynervous system inflammationnonhumanpriority journalreverse transcription polymerase chain reactionRNA extractionsex differencespatial memorySynaptophysin geneworking memoryY-maze test
%K Adolescent
%K Adolescent Behavior
%X Early life adversities during childhood (such as maltreatment abuse neglect or parental deprivation) may increase the vulnerability to cognitive disturbances and emotional disorders in both adolescence and adulthood. Maternal separation (MS) is a widely used model to study stress-related changes in brain and behavior in rodents. In this study we investigated the effect of MS (postnatal day 2–14 3 ​h/day) in both female and male adolescent mice. Specifically we evaluated (i) the spatial working memory anxiety and depressive-like behavior (ii) the hippocampal synaptic gene expression and (iii) the hippocampal neuroinflammatory response. Our results show that MS significantly increased depressive-like behavior in adolescent female mice and altered the spatial memory in adolescent male mice. In addition MS led to decreased expression of genes related to synaptic function (5ht6r Synaptophysin and Cox-2) and induced an exacerbated microglial activation in dentate gyrus (DG) CA1 and CA3. However while the levels of hippocampal inflammatory cytokines were not modified by MS they did follow a sex-specific expression in adolescent mice. Taken together our results suggest that MS induces long-term changes in hippocampal microglia and synaptic gene expression alters the spatial memory and induces depressive-like behavior in the adolescent mice in a sex-specific manner.
%R doi:10.1016/j.bbih.2020.100142

