%M rayyan-1166385378
%T Epigenetic modifications of GABAergic interneurons contribute to deficits in adult hippocampus neurogenesis and depression-like behavior in prenatally stressed mice
%D 2020
%8 2020
%J International Journal of Neuropsychopharmacology
%V 23
%N 4
%P 274-285
%A Zhong H., Rong J., Zhu C., Liang M., Li Y., Zhou R.
%K genetic analyzerconfocal laser scanning microscopeigh-capacity cDNA Reverse Transcription kitimmunochemistry analyzermicroscopemicrotomepolymerase chain reaction systemprotein isolation kitRNA purification kit4 aminobutyric acidbroxuridineDNA (cytosine 5) methyltransferase 1DNA methyltransferaseglutamate decarboxylaseglutamate decarboxylase 67neuron specific nuclear proteinphenobarbitalpicrotoxinadultanimal cellanimal experimentanimal modelanimal tissuearticlebehaviorbehavioral scienceBrdU assaycell proliferationcell survivalchromatin immunoprecipitationcontrolled studydensitometrydepressionDNA methylationdown regulationepigenetic modificationexperimental behavioral testfemalefluorescenceforced swim testGABAergic systemgene overexpressionhippocampusimmobilityimmunoblottingimmunofluorescenceimmunohistochemistryinterneuronlocomotionmousemRNA expression levelnervous system developmentnonhumanopen field testoptical densityphysiological stresspregnancyprenatal stressprotein expressionprotein expression levelreal time reverse transcription polymerase chain reactionRNA extractiontail suspension testWestern blottingABI Prism 7300 Sequence Detection System
%K Interneurons
%K Mice
%K Hippocampus
%X Background: Prenatal stress (PRS) is considered a risk factor for depressive disorder. Adult hippocampal neurogenesis is believed to play a role in the regulation of affective behaviors. GABAergic interneuron is a key modulator in adult hippocampal neurogenesis. Growing evidence indicates that PRS has adverse effects on adult hippocampal neurogenesis and DNA epigenetic modifications of the GABAergic system. The aim of this study was to investigate whether epigenetic GABAergic dysfunction participates in the negative impact of PRS on adult hippocampal neurogenesis and related emotional behaviors. Methods: Behavioral tests were used to explore PRS-induced depression-like behaviors of adult female mice. Immunohistochemistry staining real-time reverse transcription-polymerase chain reaction western blot and chromatin immunoprecipitation were employed to detect adult neurogenesis and epigenetic changes of the GABAergic system in the hippocampus of PRS mice. Results: PRS mice developed a depression phenotype accompanied by the inhibited maturation of hippocampal newborn neurons. Compared with control mice PRS mice showed decreased expression of glutamic acid decarboxylase 67 at the mRNA and protein levels. GABAA receptor agonist phenobarbital could rectify the decrease of 5-bromo-2-deoxyuridine/neuronal nuclei double-positive (BrdU+/NeuN+) cells in PRS mice. PRS mice also showed increased expression of DNA methyltransferase 1 and increased binding of DNA methyltransferase 1 to glutamic acid decarboxylase 67 promoter region. The treatment with DNA methyltransferase 1 inhibitor 5-aza-deoxycytidine restored the decrease of BrdU+/NeuN+ cells and depression-like behaviors in PRS mice via improving GABAergic system. Conclusions: The present results indicate that epigenetic changes of the GABAergic system are responsible for adult hippocampus neurogenesis and depression-like behaviors in PRS mice.
%R doi:10.1093/ijnp/pyaa020

