%P 942-949
%A Mizoguchi H., Fukumoto K., Sakamoto G., Jin S., Toyama A., Wang T., Suzumura A., Sato J.
%K fluoxetineadultadult animalallodyniaanimal behavioranimal experimentanimal modelanimal tissueanxietyanxiety disorderarticlechronic constriction injurychronic paincontrolled studydepressiondevelopmental disorderdisease exacerbationemotionmalematernal deprivationmicrogliamouseneuropathic painnociceptionnonhumanpriority journalrisk factortreatment duration
%K Neuralgia
%X Psychological stresses such as social loss and separation during childhood induce hardship referred to as emotional pain. These experiences are well-documented risk factors for the development of physical pain in adulthood. However the underlying neuronal mechanisms of this exacerbation of pain are largely unknown and consequently there is no effective pharmacotherapy. In this study we sought to determine whether infant maternal separation (MS) contributes to aggravation of neuropathic pain in adult mice. MS increased anxiety- and depression-like behavioral responses to adult stress. In MS animals chronic constriction injury (CCI) heightened the sensory dimension of chronic pain relative to that of control mice. However MS mice treated with fluoxetine for 4 weeks after MS did not exhibit augmentation of allodynia and their emotional response was attenuated. Microglia were more abundant in the spinal cord in MS/CCI mice than in control/CCI mice. These results suggest that emotional impairment is related to augmentation of neuropathic pain and that dysfunction of microglial activation contributes to heightened pain sensitivity.
%R doi:10.1016/j.bbr.2018.06.015

