%A Papilloud A., Guillot de Suduiraut I., Zanoletti O., Grosse J., Sandi C.
%K cannabinoid 1 receptorcorticosteronemitochondrial proteintranslocase of outer mitochondrial membrane 20adulthoodaggressionanimal behavioranimal cellanimal experimentanimal modelanimal tissuearticleboxingbrain mitochondrioncentral nucleus (amygdala)controlled studycoping behaviorcorticosterone blood levelenergy metabolismex vivo studyexploratory behaviorfightinggene expressiongene expression levelhabituationlatent periodmalemental diseasemitochondrial respirationnonhumannucleus accumbensnucleus accumbens shellplaypubertyresident-intruder testphysiological stressupregulation
%K Nucleus Accumbens
%K Adolescent
%K Amygdala
%X Play fighting is a highly rewarding behavior that helps individuals to develop social skills. Early-life stress has been shown to alter play fighting in rats and hamsters as well as to increase aggressive behaviors at adulthood. However it is not known whether individual differences in stress-induced play fighting are related to differential developmental trajectories towards adult aggression. To address this question we used a rat model of peripubertal stress (PPS)-induced psychopathology that involves increased aggression at adulthood. We report that indeed PPS leads to enhanced play fighting at adolescence. Using a stratification approach we identify individuals with heightened levels of play fighting as the ones that show abnormal forms of aggression at adulthood. These animals showed as well a rapid habituation of their corticosterone responsiveness to repeated stressor exposure at peripuberty. They also showed a striking increase in mitochondrial function in the amygdala—but not nucleus accumbens—when tested ex vivo. Conversely low but not high players displayed increased expression of the CB1 cannabinoid receptor in the nucleus accumbens shell. Our results highlight adolescence as a potential critical period in which aberrant play fighting is linked to the emergence of adult aggression. They also point at brain energy metabolism during adolescence as a possible target to prevent adult aggression.
%R doi:10.1038/s41398-018-0215-6

