%A Ordyan N. E., Smolenskii I. V., Pivina S. G., Akulova V. K.
%K corticosteroneadultadult animalanimal experimentanimal modelanxietyanxious depressive statearticlecontrolled studydepressiondisease modelexperimental modelfemaleforced swim testhypothalamus hypophysis adrenal systemimmobilization stressmalematernal stressnegative feedbacknonhumanposttraumatic stress disorderprenatal stressratrestressphysiological stressstress restress model
%K Rats
%K Stress Disorders, Post-Traumatic
%X The formation of the anxious-depressive state in a "stress- restress" model was studied in adult prenatally stressed male rats this being an experimental analog of human post-traumatic stress disorder. Male rats born to control females and to females subjected on days 15-19 of pregnancy to daily 1-h restraint stress were subjected to severe combined stress with subsequent restress after seven days. Both control and prenatally stressed males formed a pathological state 10 days after restress with increases in the levels of anxiety and depressive behavior as well as inhibition of the stress-related activity of the hypothalamohypophyseal-adrenocortical system due to activation of pathological negative feedback. However the signs of post-stress pathology were longer-lasting in prenatally stressed animals as evidenced by a decrease in the basal corticosterone level and an increase in anxiety still present 20 days after restress. These results lead to the conclusion that prenatal stress promotes the development of major behavioral and hormonal pathology in the stress-restress paradigm in adult animals. © 2014 Springer Science+Business Media.
%R doi:10.1007/s11055-014-9966-6

