%T Adaptations of stress coping strategies in the peripartum period: Regulation by oxytocin and prolactin?
%D 2005
%8 2005
%J Journal fur Neurologie Neurochirurgie und Psychiatrie
%V 6
%N 3
%P 16-21
%A Neumann I. D.
%K corticosteronecorticotropinhydrocortisoneoxytocinprolactinadaptive behavioraggressionanxietyarticlebirthbreast milkcoping behaviorcorticosterone releasecorticotropin releaseemotional stressfeedback systemhormonal regulationhormone receptor interactionhormone synthesishumanhydrocortisone releasehypophysishypothalamushypothalamus hypophysis adrenal systemlactationmilk ejectionlimbic cortexmaternal behaviorneuroendocrine systemnonhumanoxytocin releasepregnancyprolactin releasepuerperiumreflexreproductionphysiological stresstranquilizing activity
%K Prolactin
%K Oxytocin
%K MSH Release-Inhibiting Hormone
%X In pregnancy and lactation a variety of adaptations were described with respect to emotional and neuroendocrine stress coping strategies in animals (rats mice sheep) and humans. In particular the responsiveness of the hypothalamo-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis is attenuated exemplified as reduced adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) and corticosterone/cortisol secretory responses. In addition in rats an enhanced level of anxiety-related behaviour has been seen at the end of pregnancy whereas reduced anxiety is accompanied by increased maternal aggression in lactation. Changes in feedforward and feedback mechanisms regulating the activity of the HPA axis at limbic hypothalamic and pituitary levels seem to be responsible for these adaptations. Furthermore increased involvement of relevant neuropeptides like oxytocin and prolactin are discussed. Both peptides fulfill classical functions as reproductive hormones when released into blood and an increased activity of both the intracerebral oxytocin and prolactin systems in the peripartum period (increased synthesis release and receptor binding in hypothalamic and limbic brain regions) indicates a significant function in these behavioural and physiological changes seen at this time. Thus brain oxytocin was found to inhibit the basal and stress-induced HPA axis activity and acts as an endogenous anxiolytics in female and male rats and in pregnant and lactating rats respectively. With respect to intracerebral prolactin an anxiolytic action could be found in male and female rats regardless of the reproductive state. Stress responses as a result of acute or chronic stressor exposure impair reproductive functions. Therefore we hypothesise that oxytocin and prolactin are released into blood and within the brain in order to act synergistically to (i) regulate physiological reproductive functions (birth milk synthesis and let down) and to stimulate respective maternal behaviour and (ii) to inhibit neuroendocrine and behavioural stress responses which exert negative effects on reproduction.
%R doi:

