%P 75-80
%A Rimondini R., Ågren G., Börjesson S., Sommer W., Heilig M.
%K corticosteronediazepamanimal experimentarticlecontrolled studycorticosterone blood leveldisease modelfemalehormone responseimmobilization stresslocomotionmaze testnonhumanphenotypeprenatal periodpriority journalratskin temperaturesocial behaviortail
%K Rats
%X Prenatal restraint stress (PS) has been suggested as an attractive chronic model of anxiety. Here we characterized the behavioral and autonomic responsivity to acute stress exposure in adult PS subjects. In Wistar rats locomotor activity as well as spontaneous behavior in an established animal model of anxiety the elevated plus-maze was unaffected by PS. However the anxiogenic-like response normally seen on the plus-maze following a restraint stress was markedly potentiated in adult PS subjects despite indistinguishable corticosterone responses. In addition we assessed the tail skin temperature response to a mild social stressor transient social mixing. The diazepam-sensitive late phase of the temperature response was markedly potentiated in adult PS subjects. In summary PS induces a persistent phenotype of increased behavioral and autonomic sensitivity to stress. This paradigm might serve as an attractive screening model for anti-anxiety compounds. © 2002 Elsevier Science B.V. All rights reserved.
%R doi:10.1016/S0166-4328(02)00273-5

