%K Analgesics, Opioid
%K Rats
%K Receptors, Opioid
%X The possibility was investigated that specific opioid receptor types might selectively alter the production of isolation-induced ultrasonic vocalizations. Intracisternal injections of μ δ and κ opioid receptor agonists were administered to isolated 10-day-old rat pups. The μ receptor agonist [D-Ala2-NMe-Phe4-Gly-ol]-enkephalin (DAMGO) and δ receptor agonist [D-Pen2 D-Pen5]-enkephalin (DPDPE) both reduced the rate of isolation-induced ultrasonic calling in the absence of sedation. The κ receptor agonist U50488 had the opposite effect significantly raising the rate of vocalization. Fourteen-day-old pups with a larger δ receptor population showed a greater sensitivity to DPDPE than was seen in the younger animals.
%R doi:10.1016/0165-3806(91)90185-L

