%0      SO  - EmbaseMedline
%M rayyan-1166386796
%T Early weaning predisposes rats to exacerbated activity-stress ulcer formation
%D 1985
%8 1985
%J Physiology and Behavior
%V 34
%N 6
%P 907-909
%A Glavin G. B., Pare W. P.
%K animal experimentcentral nervous systemepidemiologymaternal deprivationmortalitynonhumanpriority journalpsychological aspectratstomachphysiological stressstress ulcer
%K Rats
%X Rats were weaned early (Day 15) normally (Day 21) or late (Day 27). When they reached 200 g (±10) they were placed into the activity-stress paradigm. Half the rats in each weaning group were housed in activity-wheel cages and fed only 1 hr each day. The remaining rats served as food-yoked home cage controls and were fed only that amount of food eaten by their whell-housed partners. Early weaned rats ran more than normally or late weaned animals. Early weaned rats also died at a faster rate and exhibited a significantly greater cumulative ulcer length than did normally or late weaned rats although ulcer incidence did not differ between the groups. No ulcers occurred in home cage control rats. Early maternal deprivation may generally predispose rats to stress-induced gastrointestinal disease whether or not such disease has an acid etiology.
%R doi:10.1016/0031-9384(85)90012-5

%0      SO  - EmbaseMedline
%M rayyan-1166386799
%T The mother-litter relationship in developmental rat studies: Cannibalism vs caring
%D 1984
%8 1984
%J Developmental Psychobiology
%V 17
%N 3
%P 255-262
%A DeSantis D. T., Schmaltz L. W.
%K animal experimentcannibalismcentral nervous systemfetusgestation periodlettermaternal behaviormaternal carenewbornnonhumanperinatal periodpregnancyratphysiological stress
%K Rats
%X This incidence of cannabalism was studied in a series of developmental studies (on neonatal lesions prenatal stress prenatal surgery unoperated controls) in which rats were manipulated during either the prenatal or early postnatal periods. Cannabalism during the preweaning period occurred in from 0 to 35% of the litters depending on the treatment group. It was found in most cases however that mothers who cannabalized were also good caretakers of their remaining pups. The same litters were also involved in preweaning tests of mother-young interactions to determine if experimental treatments altered the maternal-filial relationship. It was found that as early as postnatal Day 4 nursing episodes were predominantly pup-initiated in the lesioned litters but mother-initiated in control litters.
%R doi:

